---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { stats } from '../lib/antinet.ts';
import { stats as divisionStats } from '../lib/divisions.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const routes = [
  {
    href: '/rules',
    label: 'Filing rules',
    blurb:
      'How an address is computed, and the one decision you make every time you file: does this card continue the one in my hand, or dig into it?',
    when: 'Start here if you have a card and nowhere to put it.',
  },
  {
    href: '/core',
    label: 'The core disciplines',
    blurb: `${divisionStats.divisions} disciplines across five drawers, each with a scope note. The canonical list — nothing in it depends on whose card box it is, so it can be adopted whole.`,
    when: 'Start here if you are building the skeleton of a new Antinet.',
  },
  {
    href: '/extending',
    label: 'Extending the core',
    blurb:
      'How to use that list as it is, and how to earn a row of your own: the units digit, the three-card test, the scope-note recipe, and what must never be renumbered.',
    when: 'Start here if the list is missing something you care about.',
  },
  {
    href: '/extensions',
    label: 'My extensions',
    blurb: `The ${divisionStats.personal} sub-disciplines my own cards paid for, each with the card that would not file anywhere else. A worked example of the method, not an addition to the list.`,
    when: 'Start here if you want to see the method actually applied.',
  },
  {
    href: '/find',
    label: 'Find the discipline',
    blurb:
      'Four questions, and the one rule that settles most ties: mechanism files with the science, action files with the practice.',
    when: 'Start here if you have a card and two disciplines both look right.',
  },
  {
    href: '/outline',
    label: 'The numbered outline',
    blurb: `All ${stats.total.toLocaleString('en')} addresses of the source outline, mapped onto the disciplines. The scheme at full scale, and the evidence the table covers the field.`,
    when: 'Start here if you want to stress-test the scheme.',
  },
  {
    href: '/hubs',
    label: 'Hub cards',
    blurb:
      'What to do when your interests cut across the numbering — which they will, because an interest is a practice and the table is made of fields of study.',
    when: 'Start here once you have enough cards to lose one.',
  },
];

const questions = [
  ['Can four digits really hold everything?', '/core'],
  ['What number does this card get?', '/rules'],
  ['Two disciplines both fit — which do I pick?', '/find'],
  ['How do I file a card between two others?', '/rules'],
  ['The list has nothing for what I care about', '/extending'],
  ['When have I earned a new discipline?', '/extending'],
  ['What if my topic is not a discipline?', '/hubs'],
  ['Is any of this list just your preferences?', '/extensions'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Start"
  description="A living implementation manual for the Antinet Zettelkasten — the practical questions of running one, answered in one place from a worked implementation."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">A living implementation manual</p>
    <h1>The Antinet, answered in one place</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Running an Antinet Zettelkasten raises practical questions that most sources either skip
      or answer in passing, scattered across a dozen half-agreeing posts. This site answers
      them from a worked implementation: a complete numbering scheme, applied at full scale,
      with the reasoning left in.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="figures" aria-label="Scale of the worked example">
    <div><b class="ad">{divisionStats.divisions}</b><span>Disciplines</span></div>
    <div><b class="ad">{divisionStats.drawers}</b><span>Drawers</span></div>
    <div><b class="ad">{stats.total.toLocaleString('en')}</b><span>Terms mapped</span></div>
    <div><b class="ad">{stats.maxDepth}</b><span>Levels deep</span></div>
  </section>

  <h2>What is here</h2>
  <p>
    The taxonomy is published in two tiers, and the split is the thing to notice first:
    {' '}<a href={`${base}/core`}>a canonical list</a> anyone can adopt whole, and
    {' '}<a href={`${base}/extensions`}>one filer's own rows</a> kept off it. The digits are laid
    out so the second can grow for decades without moving anything in the first.
  </p>
  <div class="routes">
    {
      routes.map((r) => (
        <a class="route" href={`${base}${r.href}`}>
          <h3>{r.label}</h3>
          <p>{r.blurb}</p>
          <p class="when">{r.when}</p>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>If you arrived with a question</h2>
  <ul class="qs">
    {
      questions.map(([q, href]) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`${base}${href}`}>{q}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>The one idea to take away</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>The four digits are the classification. Everything after the slash is
      position, not meaning.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Which means the branch never has to be <em>correct</em> — only unique and stable. Almost
      every difficulty people report with a numbered Zettelkasten comes from trying to make the
      address carry meaning it was never built to hold. The number's job is to be a pointer
      other cards can rely on forever. Finding things is a different job, done by hub cards and
      a keyword index.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="foot-note">
    The worked example numbers an outline of academic disciplines, but the scheme is not about
    academia — it is a demonstration at a scale large enough to break a system that does not
    work. Your own Antinet will file mostly your own thinking, branching off these anchors.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .figures {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1.75rem;
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .figures div {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
  }
  .figures b {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .figures span {
    font: 500 0.7rem/1 var(--sans);
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .routes {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  .route {
    display: block;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brass);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .route:hover {
    background: var(--tint);
  }
  .route h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    color: var(--brass);
  }
  .route p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.91rem;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .route .when {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.83rem;
  }
  .qs {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 3px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .qs li {
    background: var(--surface);
    max-width: none;
  }
  .qs a {
    display: block;
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--serif);
  }
  .qs a:hover {
    background: var(--tint);
    color: var(--brass);
  }
  .foot-note {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.89rem;
  }
</style>
